<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<!--
  The Trigly mark with nothing behind it. This is `<application android:icon>`,
  and it is the one icon in this app that cannot follow the chosen colour
  scheme.

  WHY IT CANNOT FOLLOW. From Android 12 the platform draws an icon inside every
  toast, and SystemUI picks that icon itself: it reads the package's
  `ApplicationInfo` (`getApplicationInfoAsUser`) and loads
  `IconDrawableFactory.getBadgedIcon(appInfo, user)`. That is the
  `<application>` icon. It is never an `activity-alias` icon, and the nine
  aliases are the whole mechanism by which the launcher icon follows the
  scheme, so none of them is reachable from the toast. `PackageManager` offers
  no way to change an application icon at run time either: it has
  `getApplicationIcon` and no setter. See `ToastAction` for the full finding.

  So the mark loses its plate here rather than wear a plate in the wrong
  colour. Three decisions follow from that, and each has a reason that is not
  visible in the shapes below.

  NOT AN ADAPTIVE ICON, unlike `ic_launcher.xml` and its eight preset
  siblings. A plate is what the adaptive canvas exists for. Without one, two
  of its rules turn into faults. Its safe zone is a 66dp circle on the 108
  board, which holds the mark to the 0.94 scale `ic_launcher_foreground.xml`
  explains, and 0.94 of the board reads as a small mark adrift in a large
  empty box the moment the plate is gone. And `LauncherIcons`
  `wrapIconDrawableWithShadow`, which `IconDrawableFactory` calls on the way
  to the toast, draws a blurred shadow of the icon *mask* for an adaptive
  icon: a transparent adaptive background therefore renders as a grey rounded
  square behind nothing at all, on every Android 13 and later. That same
  function returns a plain drawable untouched, which is what this is.

  The cost of that choice, stated rather than hidden, and it is two OS
  versions wide. SystemUI changed how it loads this icon in Android 13.
  Android 12 and 12L use the launcher's own `IconFactory`
  (`createBadgedIconBitmap(icon, user, shrinkNonAdaptiveIcons = true)`), which
  shrinks a non-adaptive icon into an adaptive wrapper whose background is
  `BaseIconFactory.DEFAULT_WRAPPER_BACKGROUND`, plain white. So on exactly
  those two releases the mark does arrive on a white plate, smaller than it is
  drawn here. Android 13 and later replaced that call with the framework's
  `IconDrawableFactory`, which adds no wrapper at all. The trade was made in
  that direction on purpose: the plated version is wrong on two releases and
  still legible, 3.32:1 orange on white; the adaptive version is wrong on
  every release from 13 up, which is both gate devices and nearly every phone
  in use.

  THE SCALE IS `ic_notification.xml`'s, 99 of 108 units, and for the same
  reason: an icon with no plate should fill its box. The mark spans y 29..79,
  so 50 of 108 units, and 99/50 = 1.98. See that file for the arithmetic.

  INK IN LIGHT, WHITE IN DARK, AND NOT THE BRAND ORANGE. The mark has no
  plate here, so it sits straight on the toast frame, which is
  `?android:attr/colorSurface`: `#FAF8FF` in light and `#2F3036` in dark on
  the platform's own palette. One colour would have to serve both grounds, and
  the brand orange is the best single answer at 3.16:1 and 3.96:1, over the
  3:1 WCAG floor for a graphic but no more than that. A `values-night` pair
  needs no compromise at all: ink `#17100C` is 16.9:1 on the light ground and
  white is 11.4:1 on the dark one.

  The pair is safe to rely on here, and that is worth stating because it is
  not obvious. SystemUI loads this resource in SystemUI's process and resolves
  the night qualifier against SystemUI's own configuration. That is the same
  system dark mode that chooses the toast background, so the mark and the
  ground it lands on are decided by one setting and cannot disagree. What the
  qualifier does NOT follow is the app's own scheme choice, which is correct:
  the toast is the platform's surface, not the app's page.

  The cost is on Android 12 and 12L only, where the white wrapper plate
  described above appears. In dark mode on exactly those two releases the mark
  is white on that white plate and cannot be seen. Neither gate level is one
  of them, an unreadable toast icon loses nothing but decoration, and the
  alternative was a mark that is merely adequate on every release instead of
  right on nearly all of them. `AppMarkContrastTest` holds the ratios.
-->
<vector xmlns:android="http://schemas.android.com/apk/res/android"
    android:width="48dp"
    android:height="48dp"
    android:viewportWidth="108"
    android:viewportHeight="108">

    <group
        android:pivotX="54"
        android:pivotY="54"
        android:scaleX="1.98"
        android:scaleY="1.98">

        <!-- The bar of the T. -->
        <path
            android:fillColor="@color/ic_app_mark_foreground"
            android:pathData="M32,29 h44 v12 h-44 z" />

        <!-- The stem, running into the arrowhead rather than stopping short. -->
        <path
            android:fillColor="@color/ic_app_mark_foreground"
            android:pathData="M49,41 h10 v22 h-10 z" />

        <!-- The arrowhead. -->
        <path
            android:fillColor="@color/ic_app_mark_foreground"
            android:pathData="M42,63 h24 l-12,16 z" />
    </group>
</vector>
